<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sana'i</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1131</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Sanai, Hakim Sana'i, Sana'i Ghaznavi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sana'i was a Persian poet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> who lived at the court of the late Ghaznavid sultans. After early panegyric verse he turned to religious and mystical poetry, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadiqat-al-haqiqa">Hadiqat al-Haqiqa</a>, dedicated to Bahram Shah, was the first great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> didactic poem in Persian. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a> and other later mystical poets acknowledged him as a master.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sanai.jpg" alt="Sana'i" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%22Hakim_Sanai_addressing_the_Sultan_of_Ghazna%22_Folio_from_Hadiqat_al_Haqiqa_(The_Garden_of_Truth_and_The_Path_to_Trek)_MS_OR._1651,_fol._1b.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
